        "explanation": "Hamlet reasons that a man killed while praying may be forgiven and go to heaven, whereas his own father died unshriven. He wants Claudius damned, not saved, so he vows to wait for a sinful moment. The scene's irony is that Claudius admits his prayer failed — \"Words without thoughts never to heaven go\" — so Hamlet's careful restraint was based on a false assumption.",
